German company Siemens has announced it  will not be backing out of a deal to  provide infrastructure for a  controversial coal mine in Australia  that's despite growing pressure from  environmental groups who linked carbon  emissions to the bush fires devastating  large parts of the country there have  been protests at home and abroad since  it signed the deal in December but  Siemens as boss says there's no legally  and economically responsible way out of  the agreement to supply rail  infrastructure for the Adani mine well  for more on this let's speak to David  Ritter he's the CEO of Greenpeace sister  Australia Pacific and also the author of  a book about the Adani coal mine and he  joins us now on skype David let me start  with some promises from Siemens seaman  says it supports the Paris agreement and  it's also promised I see to be carbon  neutral by 2030 how does that square  with this decision  well Siemens has given itself an  enormous kick in the guts today it has  made a disastrous decision for its own  reputation and it's hollow commitment to  the Paris agreement goes to the heart of  that the development of the Adani coal  mine is completely inconsistent in  spirit with the Paris agreement so it  beggars belief that Siemens can actually  think it is committed to Paris while it  is supporting the construction of this  coal mine David I see I see that the CEO  of Siemens Joe Kaiser is saying that the  rail contract would be going ahead  regardless even if they pulled out so  are you pushing for more of a symbolic  gesture from Siemens then no absolutely  not I mean that is the drug dealers  defense  it's what anyone who is doing something  wrong for a profit always is in the  circumstances where they're caught out  the truth is that Siemens is providing  crucial services to the Adani company  and that it would be a body blow to the  project if Siemens pulled out now  Siemens should withdraw its contractual  commitment because of their own  commitment to the Paris agreement but  more so because the vast majority of  Australians are against the Adhan EEMA  I'm going ahead and particularly at the  moment in the context where as you say  Australia is being ravaged by terrible  unprecedented fires which are themselves  being made worse and generated by global  warming which the scientists are being  very very clear about and that is driven  in turn by coal above all else  so for Siemens to be supporting the  construction of this Adani mine at the  same time as Australia is literally on  fire is simply appalling corporate  practice  David you say Australians are an T the  Adani coal mine but I see the government  has quite a different a different stance  the Resources Minister wrote to Kaiser  last month pleading for him and I quote  not to be intimidated by the noisy  anti-coal minority targeting the mine  project and he wrote if the protestors  achieved their goals of ending coal  mining by bullying companies into  submission the result would be millions  more people without a home access to  electricity and without as much hope as  they otherwise could have what's your  response to that well that is an absurd  selective reading of things a majority  of the Australian people want climate  action that is very clear a majority of  the Australian people oppose the Adani  mine and it is not going to be old dirty  technologies like coal that provide  energy democracy and power to those who  need it it's going to be the new  decentralized technologies particularly  solar so I'm sorry but that is a very  self-serving line and it is a shame that  Siemens have bought it because the truth  is that Australia needs to move beyond  exporting and burning coal as fast as  possible to make good on our nation's  commitment to the Paris agreement and to  go will beyond that as a as a world we  have to turn away from coal as fast as  we possibly can so that we have a  livable climate in the future and I'm  afraid Siemens has really delivered a  body blow to it  reputation with this announcement today  and pressure will simply continue to  increase on the company from the tens of  thousands of people who are appalled in  Germany in Australia and around the  world as the lead up to the company's  AGM in February continues really this is  a sad day for Siemens David Ritter there  the CEO of Greenpeace Australia Pacific  thank you for joining us on al-jazeera  David
 

